  "description": "Select token positions for bidirectional cloze mask replacement and record ground truth targets.\n\nMasked Language Modeling forces transformers to learn deep bidirectional representations by reconstructing hidden tokens from surrounding context.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nx_i \\leftarrow \\operatorname{[MASK]} \\quad \\text{with probability } p_{\\text{mask}}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Scan sequence tokens and determine masking candidates\n2. Replace designated tokens with mask_token_id\n3. Record original tokens at masked coordinates for cross-entropy supervision\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'masked_ids', 'masked_positions', and 'original_tokens'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Masked Language Modeling\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNatural language consists of discrete words and symbols arranged in sequential order. To process text with neural networks, we convert words into continuous embedding vectors and model their grammatical and semantic relationships. **Masked Language Modeling** is a core sequence-processing mechanism used in modern Transformer and LLM architectures.\n\nMasked Language Modeling forces transformers to learn deep bidirectional representations by reconstructing hidden tokens from surrounding context. In this problem, the goal is to: select token positions for bidirectional cloze mask replacement and record ground truth targets.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nx_i \\leftarrow \\operatorname{[MASK]} \\quad \\text{with probability } p_{\\text{mask}}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Scan sequence tokens and determine masking candidates\n2. **Phase 2**: Replace designated tokens with mask_token_id\n3. **Phase 3**: Record original tokens at masked coordinates for cross-entropy supervision\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Self-Attention Mechanics**: Scaled dot-product attention computes all-to-all token interactions dynamically, allowing tokens to gather context from the entire sentence simultaneously.\n* **Position Encodings**: Because self-attention is permutation-invariant, positional encodings (like sinusoidal or Rotary Position Embeddings) inject word order into the representations.\n* **Vocabulary & Subwords**: Algorithms like BPE tokenize text into frequent subwords, allowing models to represent rare words without requiring an infinite vocabulary.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: token_ids = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60], mask_token_id = 999, mask_prob = 0.2\n\nOutput: {\"masked_ids\": [10, 999, 30, 40, 999, 60], \"masked_positions\": [1, 4], \"original_tokens\": [20, 50]}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated nlp_masked_language_modeling under standard test conditions."
